Morning kickoff for {{ display_name }}: create today's daily note and get the day moving.

Arguments: $ARGUMENTS

**This is not a note-generation command — the note is one output of it, not the point of it.** The point is: get today's read-only data on screen fast, ask the three things only a human can answer, and pick up any genuinely new tasks from overnight — without paying a single expensive model call for things that are pure formatting. Renamed from `/daily-note` 2026-09-07 (see the vault plan `Projects/lios/Plans/Daily Kickoff — Rebuilding Daily Note as a Morning Ritual.md` for the full audit and decisions behind this shape).

## Claims carry their source

Almost everything in this note is pasted verbatim from `system_daily_brief(render=true)` — server-rendered markdown fragments, each sourced at generation time. **That rule now holds by construction for those fragments: don't add, strip, or recompute anything inside them.** The only things you compute yourself are Track B's answers (coffee, commute, the catch-all) and Track C's confirmed tasks — write those plainly, no marker needed, since you're the source.

**Track C2's fragments (Morning read, the Pulse asides) are commentary, not facts — and that distinction is the whole point of splitting it out.** They're pasted the same way (verbatim, own subagent, own instructions below), but they exist to add judgement the rendered fragments deliberately don't carry, never to restate a number a rendered fragment already gives. If a Pulse aside ever disagrees with its matching fact block on a figure, the fact block is right and the aside is a bug in the subagent's instructions. **The Email/WhatsApp digests Track C2 used to produce are retired as of 2026-09-10 (lios#192, #193)** — `## Conversations` (Phase 2) is built from Track C's TriageList List 2 instead, which reads whole conversations rather than scoring messages in isolation.

**Prior daily notes are not a data source for anything in this note.** Read them only if you need continuity for the catch-all conversation — never to fill in a number or status today's brief doesn't hand you directly.

## 0. Check whether today's note already exists

`vault/{{ daily_notes_dir }}YYYY-MM-DD.md`. **If it exists, stop and say so** — point at `/checkin` instead ("Today's note is already up — run `/checkin` to update it, or `/checkin <section>` for just one part"). `/kickoff` is a create-once ritual; re-entry during the day is `/checkin`'s job. If it doesn't exist, continue.

## Phase 0 — fan out

Fire Track A, Track B and Track C together. None of them wait on each other. **Track C2 is the exception** — it needs Track B's answers, so it's spawned once those land rather than at the top of the phase (see Track C2, below); intake (Track C) itself still starts immediately, in parallel with Track A and B.

### Track A — background, no model

1. **Vault guard.** `python3 .tools/vault_guard.py` (Bash, from the vault root, no arguments — canonical copy also at `core/scripts/vault_guard.py` in the dev repo). Local, instant, no server round-trip.
   - Exit 0 → nothing to add.
   - Exit 1 → capture stdout verbatim. It names every `.sync-conflict-*` file (with a verdict on which side is truncated) and every 0-byte `.md` note. This becomes a `## Vault guard` heading in the note — never silently swallow a finding.
2. **One `system_daily_brief` call, and only one.** `{"render": true, "since": "<mtime of the last daily note, ISO>", "sections": [...]}`. Omit `since` if there's no previous note (server defaults the window). Build `sections` from `_meta.preferences["daily_note.sections"]` (see the table below) — always include `alerts`; include `freshness` only if `daily_note.show_freshness` is set; include `listening` only if `daily_note.show_listening` is set.
   - **Two vocabularies, one call.** The preference lists sections by their note names (`today`, `house`, `pulse`, …); the `rendered` object is keyed by fragment names (`calendar`, `consumables`, `pulse`, …). The server accepts either in `sections` (`calendar`→`today`, `consumables`→`house`, `listening`→`pulse`), so pass whichever you have. **After the call, check `_meta`: `sections_unknown` must be `[]` and `sections_enabled` must be non-empty.** An empty `sections_enabled` means nothing was fetched, and every fragment will come back `_Not measured today._` — that's a bad request, not a data gap (it produced a `## Today` reading "not measured" on a day with seven meetings, 2026-09-08). Fix the names and call again rather than pasting the empty result.
   - The response's `rendered` object carries one markdown fragment per requested section — `alerts`, `pulse`, `coffee`, `transport`, `consumables`, `snags`, `calendar`, `listening`, `freshness`. **Every requested key is present in `rendered`; its *value* is what tells you whether to render anything.** A non-empty value already carries its own `##` heading (see `app/integrations/system/brief_render.py`'s module docstring for the exact heading text per section — e.g. `alerts` renders under `## System Alerts`, `consumables` under `## House`, `calendar` under `## Today`) — paste it verbatim, don't rename, rewrap, or add a heading of your own around it. **An empty string (`""`) means omit that section's heading from the note entirely** — this covers both "not configured for this user" and "ran clean, genuinely nothing to report" (a real zero, e.g. no snags captured, no coffee bag on the go, no departures on a weekend); the two look identical on purpose and neither is worth a heading.
{% if section:snags %}
   - **`rendered.snags` is always `""` through this call, permanently.** `system_daily_brief` is read-only by design and never invokes `snag_capture` (which writes), so its renderer never has anything to render. The `## Snags` section instead comes from calling `snag_capture` directly — see Track A step 3, below.
{% endif %}
   - A fragment that says `_Not measured today._` under its heading (Pulse's Sleep/Vitals/Workout lines, Alerts, Today, Data freshness can all do this) is a **third state, not an error and not a zero** — it means the source genuinely wasn't available to render *today*. Paste it as-is; never reinterpret it as "no data ever" or strip it as noise, and never let it read as a system fault (that's what `⚠️ ... unavailable` is for, on a real error).
   - `system_daily_brief` also still returns `_meta` (`preferences`, `lookback_start`, `is_weekend`, `sections_enabled`, `sources_skipped_no_data`) exactly as before — read personalisation from it, never from memory of a previous run.
   - The unfiltered/hourly-prewarmed brief can still be large; if it's spooled to a file rather than returned inline, that's expected — pull `rendered` and `_meta` with `jq`, don't read the file linearly.
{% if section:snags %}
3. **`snag_capture`** (writes) — call directly, in parallel with the brief call, when `daily_note.sections` includes `snags`. No arguments needed (idempotent against its own prior captures, default `since_days: 7`). Its result (`snags_created`, `snags: [{uid, room, description, trade}]`, `sheet_url`) is what builds `## Snags` in Phase 2 — never `rendered.snags` (see above). If `snags_created` is 0, there's nothing to render and the whole heading is omitted.
{% endif %}
4. **`inbox_pending`** — call it here, in the same parallel batch, no arguments. This is the capture queue: voice memos (with their transcript in `note`), photos (with a vision description in `note`, or `vision_error` when that failed), Strong workout exports, anything a Shortcut or the WhatsApp self-chat dropped in. Hold the result for Track C — this is the TriageList's **List 1** raw material (explicit inbox items, §4(b) of the phase contract) — these are the things the human deliberately sent in, so they outrank everything Track C builds from conversations.
5. **`conversations_since(since=<the same `since` passed to `system_daily_brief`, ISO>)`** — call it here too, same parallel batch. Deterministic grouping only (chat/thread identity + a time-gap burst window) — no embedding call, no LLM call; see `app/integrations/system/conversations.py`. Returns `{since, until, sources, burst_gap_minutes, counts, groups: [{source, key, title, participants, span, message_count, from_me_count, first_message, last_message, has_question, mentions_me, messages}]}`, one group per Gmail thread or per WhatsApp chat-burst, newest-first. **Hold the result for Track C — this is the TriageList's List 2 raw material.** Never present a `conversations_since` group's individual `messages` one at a time in the terminal readout; the group is the unit (this is the fix for lios#192 — the AGM question already answered in-thread, the belly-pain remark read out of context).

#### Personalisation — from `_meta.preferences`, read fresh every time

| Preference | Effect |
|---|---|
| `daily_note.sections` | Which `rendered` sections to request and render, in order |
| `daily_note.focus_count` | Passed through to `/tunetasks`' lock-in step — not used here, this note no longer sets Focus |
| `house.appliance_entities` | Read server-side by the `consumables` fragment — nothing for this template to do with it directly |
| `rail.direction` | Already applied server-side to `transport` |

If a preference is absent, use its default — never fall back to what a previous note showed.

### Track B — foreground, interactive

Ask all three together, in one message, right after firing Track A and Track C (don't wait for their results first). **Ask as plain text in your reply — never through the structured question widget (`AskUserQuestion`).** Questions 1 and 3 are free-text and the widget requires a fixed option list, so it fails with "Invalid tool parameters" and the user ends up answering one question at a time (2026-09-08). One plain message, three lines, one reply:

> "Morning. Three quick ones:
> 1. What are you drinking / brewing today? (or 'nothing yet')
> 2. Commuting in today? (yes/no — if yes I'll pull trains)
> 3. Anything else on your mind for today?"

Hold the answers. They land in Phase 2: the coffee answer folds into the `coffee` block (a plain note if it adds context the render doesn't have — the render already covers the mechanical bag/brew-history side), the commute answer decides whether `transport` renders at all, and the catch-all answer is the seed of `## Notes`.

If the user included content in `$ARGUMENTS` already answering one of these (e.g. `/kickoff feeling rough today, tax return is on my mind`), treat that as the answer to Q3 and only ask the other two.

### Track C — background subagent, triage (the TriageList)

**Replaces the old message-by-message intake table (lios#192, folds in #193).** Alex's own framing (2026-09-10 voice memo): "looking at individual messages... not useful. So what is useful to look at is stuff that's been explicitly sent to the inbox, and then we should look at conversations in context... here's a bunch of just FYI stuff, and then here are things where maybe we need to make a decision about whether we ingest it or not." Track C now builds that two-list **TriageList** — it does not score or present individual messages at all.

Spawn one subagent (Haiku is fine — this is matching and light judgement, not synthesis) and give it, verbatim: Track A's `inbox_pending` result and Track A's `conversations_since` result. Instructions:

> You're given two payloads: `inbox_pending` (the capture queue) and `conversations_since` (grouped WhatsApp/Gmail conversations since this morning's window). Build a single numbered **TriageList**, two lists, continuous numbering across both (List 1 first, then List 2 continuing the count — never restart at 1).
>
> **List 1 — explicit inbox items.** One row per item in `inbox_pending.items` (or whatever key holds the pending list) — number, age, kind, original filename, one-line preview/detail (same shape as the inbox table the caller already knows: the transcript for audio, the vision description for an image, `preview` for text/documents). These already carry their own routing decision (vault/corpus/archive/dismiss/issue) — you are not judging them, just listing them in order, oldest first.
>
> **List 2 — conversations in context.** One row per group in `conversations_since.groups`. First call `tasks_intake_candidates()` with defaults (no arguments) and use its `candidates[].matches` only to tag a conversation as already-in-the-ledger — **never present `tasks_intake_candidates`' individual candidates as their own list; it is a matching aid for this step, not a third list.** For each conversation group, decide **FYI** or **DECIDE**:
>
> - **DECIDE** — there is a decision to make or a thing to take on: a question addressed to Alex still open at the end of the group (`has_question` is a hint, not a verdict — read `last_message` and, if you need more, the group's own `messages`), a request, a date/commitment, a candidate task. Read the group as a conversation, not as isolated lines — the AGM question that was already answered later in the same thread is FYI, not DECIDE, precisely because reading the whole burst shows the resolution.
> - **FYI** — everything else, including: a group that already matches an open task per `tasks_intake_candidates` (say so — "already in the ledger, TASK-0404"), an ordinary exchange with nothing pending, a passing remark (e.g. an evening update at the tail of an otherwise low-key thread — don't elevate it just because it's the last line).
>
> Each List 2 row: number, source, title/chat name, participants, span (first→last), a one-line reason for the verdict. **One row per conversation group — never a merged row, never a range.**
>
> **Cap List 2 at `conversations_since`'s own `counts.total`** (it's already capped server-side at `limit`, default 30) — if `counts.total_before_limit` is larger, say so in one line ("N older conversations not shown"). Don't re-cap further yourself.
>
> Return `{list1: [...], list2: [...], until: <verbatim from tasks_intake_candidates, for the caller to advance the marker later>}`. Do not call `tasks_add`, `tasks_intake_mark`, or any inbox-routing tool yourself — you're producing the TriageList for the human to answer by number, not applying anything.

Hold the result for Phase 1.

### Track C2 — background subagent, morning read

**Spawned once Track B's three answers have landed** — not at the top of Phase 0 like the others — because the Morning read is meant to be able to say something like "commuting in, so leave by 8" and can't until the commute answer exists. It still runs in the background alongside Track A finishing up and Track C's intake pass; nothing in Phase 1 blocks on it arriving a beat later than the rest.

This is the fix for a real complaint: the pure-formatting redesign cut every judgement-tier line from the note, including the italic Pulse asides and any morning assessment — leaving 2.7 KB of facts with no commentary at all. This subagent brings that back, deliberately kept off the ~150k-char full brief that caused the original one-Haiku-call latency problem: it reads a restricted ~75 KB slice instead.

**The Email/WhatsApp digest fragments (`email`, `whatsapp`) are retired as of 2026-09-10 (lios#192).** They scored/tiered individual messages in isolation — the exact failure the TriageList's List 2 (Track C, above) replaces with thread-grouped, context-aware FYI/DECIDE triage. Nothing here re-reads mail/WhatsApp; `## Conversations` in Phase 2 is built entirely from Track C's List 2.

Spawn one Sonnet subagent (this needs real judgement — weighing what matters, not matching) with these instructions:

> Read the brief file from Track A's `system_daily_brief` call, but pull **only** these top-level keys with `jq` — do not read anything else in the file, it can run past 150k characters and everything outside this list is either already rendered elsewhere or irrelevant here: `health_summary`, `health_sleep`, `health_trends`, `lastfm_recent`, `lastfm_stats`, `calendar`, `reminders`, `attachments`, `alerts.issues`. Also call **`health_exercise_status`** yourself (it isn't in the brief file) — its `target.strength_sessions` is the current weekly floor and `on_track` already accounts for remaining days in the week, so use those fields rather than reasoning about raw workouts.
>
> You're also given Track B's three answers verbatim (coffee, commute yes/no, and the catch-all) — you can reference them in the Morning read (e.g. "commuting in, so leave time for the 8:04") but never invent a plan they didn't state.
>
> Produce a JSON object with two fields — `morning_read`, `pulse_asides` — each a markdown string (or `""` if there's genuinely nothing to say for that one):
>
> - **`morning_read`** — 3–6 lines of prose, no heading of your own (the caller adds "## Morning read"): the shape of the day, what's waiting on {{ display_name }}, one real observation. Voice: warm, direct, a little dry — like the italic Pulse asides used to read, not like a status report.
> - **`pulse_asides`** — a JSON object keyed by `recovery` (from `health_sleep`), `movement` (from `health_trends`), `exercise` (from your own `health_exercise_status` call — compare `actual.strength_sessions` against `target.strength_sessions` and note `on_track`, never a hard-coded weekly number), `listening` (from `lastfm_recent`/`lastfm_stats`) — one italic-ready line of commentary each, no leading `*` (the caller wraps it). **Every line must be trend-relative, not a single-point restatement** (lios#193 — "8,642 steps, almost exactly on the average" is what to avoid; "recovery's been climbing three days running" is the shape to hit) — compare today's reading against the recent run in `health_trends`/`lastfm_stats`, don't just restate today's number in words. **Only include a key when its source data is present and not an error** — omit the key entirely for missing/errored data rather than writing a line about it; the caller pastes each present key next to its matching Pulse fact line and simply skips the ones you didn't include. `listening` stays its own line here for its own top-level `## Listening` section in Phase 2 (not nested under Pulse — that placement was checked and kept as-is on 2026-09-10, not reopened).
>
> Rules, and they're not optional: **never restate a number a rendered fact fragment already carries** — you're adding judgement on top of facts already on screen, not repeating them. Every number you *do* write must come from the payload above or your own `health_exercise_status` call. Prior daily notes are never a source, even for continuity. If one of the keys you were told to read is missing from the payload, or holds `{"error": ...}`, don't invent content — omit that key from `pulse_asides` rather than guessing; the same applies if `health_exercise_status` itself errors.

Hold the result for Phase 2 — it isn't part of the terminal readout in Phase 1 (Track B's answers are already on screen from asking them, and the point of the readout is to move fast; the Morning read lands with the rest of the written note).

