   Rows 1–3 are Track C's List 1 (`inbox_pending`, unchanged shape from before), oldest-first. Rows 4 onward are Track C's List 2 (`conversations_since`, via Track C's FYI/DECIDE labelling) — Decide rows first, then FYI, but **the numbering is one continuous sequence across all three groups, set by Track C, never renumbered here.** FYI rows are shown for reading only — they're never part of the reply, and List 2's own cap note (if any conversations were cut by `conversations_since`'s `limit`) goes under the table as one line.

   **Table rules, learned the hard way (2026-09-08):** the table goes **inline in your reply as plain markdown** — never an artifact, never a file, never the question widget (both failed with "Invalid tool parameters" and cost a round-trip each). **One row per item, one integer per row.** No ranges, no comma lists, no merged rows — the human replies by number.

   Then ask, in one message:

   > "Triage: inbox items 1–3 — route each (e.g. `1 corpus renovation`, `2 vault Inbox/`, `3 archive`, or `dismiss`/`issue bug`/`preview`). Decide items 4–5 — reply `<n> task`, `<n> note`, `<n> ignore`, or `<n> skip` for each (e.g. `4 task`, `5 skip`). One line per number, or `all archive`/`all skip` for a whole group, or 'none' to leave everything as-is."

   Decode each numbered reply by which list the number belongs to (Track C's own numbering tells you):

   **Inbox rows** (List 1) — one tool call per item, run in parallel:
   - `corpus [tags…]` → `inbox_to_corpus` (`project_tags=[…]`, default `["tines"]`)
   - `vault <target-path>` → `inbox_to_vault` (`target=<path>`; bare `vault` defaults to `Inbox/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<original-filename-or-slug>.<ext>`)
   - `archive` → `inbox_archive`
   - `dismiss` → `inbox_dismiss`
   - `preview` → `inbox_preview` first, show full text, re-ask
   - `issue [bug|feature|other] [title…]` → this item is about lios itself (a bug or improvement in the platform, not a household matter). Run, from the shell: `gh issue create -R cograda/lios --title "<title>" --label <label> --body "<the captured note text, plus 'Captured via inbox <date>'>"`. Default `<label>` to `other` when none given. Default `<title>` to the note's first line with any leading `loops feedback -`/`lios:`/`bug:` prefix stripped, when no title is given in the reply. Then `inbox_archive` the item — it does not go to corpus or vault.

   **Decide rows** (List 2) — the verdict, applied against that conversation group:
   - `task`/`todo` → first check whether it's clearly about lios itself (a bug or improvement in Loops, the daily note, a sync daemon, an MCP tool) rather than household work — if so, file a GitHub issue the same way as an inbox `issue` (above) instead of `tasks_add`, and say so in the confirmation line. Otherwise `tasks_add(title=..., project=..., source="kickoff")`, pulling the actual detail from the conversation group (its `messages`, or `whatsapp_thread`/`gmail_thread` for more than the capped 20) into the task description — find the project via `tasks_structure()` if one fits, otherwise leave it loose.
   - `note` → no task; record the decision as a one-line entry under `## Conversations` in Phase 2 (e.g. "acknowledged, no action needed") without creating a ledger row — for a DECIDE row that turned out to need only a reply or an FYI-level note, not a task.
   - `ignore`/`dismiss` → decline; nothing written, the row is not carried forward.
   - `skip` → **not a decision** — see "Skipped rows" below. Record it as skipped, don't apply anything.

   **A number with no reply at all is also a skip** — same handling as an explicit `skip`.

   After all replies land (or if the user said "none"), call `tasks_intake_mark(until=<the until Track C returned>)` to advance the marker, regardless of how many were added — a marker that only advances on an add re-surfaces declined items every day.

   Confirm in one line: `"Triage: 2 to corpus (renovation), 1 archived, 1 task added (TASK-0409), 1 noted, 1 skipped (re-asking tomorrow)."`

### Skipped Decide rows

Per the phase contract §7/§10.3: a Decide row skipped (no reply, or explicit `skip`) is not silently dropped, and it's not re-litigated today either. **This step implements the presentation and records the choice in the note's Notes section only — persisting per-row skip state server-side (so a row skipped twice actually drops, per the design's default) is step 3 of the build order, not built yet.** For now: note in `## Notes` which rows were skipped today, and say in the confirmation line that they'll be offered again tomorrow (the re-ask-once-then-drop default from the design isn't enforced automatically yet — there's no stored `first_skipped_on` — so today's skip is always presented as "will re-ask," not "will drop," until that piece lands).

